  "title": "Adding Br₂ to ethene: the bromonium ion",
  "caption": "The approaching π bond polarises Br₂. The π electrons bond to the near bromine, which uses a lone pair to bond to the other carbon, giving a three-membered bromonium ion. Bromide then opens the ring from the opposite face, which is why addition is anti.",
  "alt": "Mechanism of bromine adding to ethene through a cyclic bromonium ion, which bromide opens from the opposite face to give 1,2-dibromoethane.",
  "description": [
    "Left: ethene and a bromine molecule, polarised δ+ on the near bromine and δ− on the far one. Three curly arrows: from the C=C bond to the near bromine; from a lone pair on that bromine back to the other carbon; and from the Br–Br bond to the far bromine.",
    "Middle: a cyclic bromonium ion, a three-membered ring of two carbons and a positively charged bromine, with a bromide ion on the opposite side. A curly arrow goes from a lone pair on bromide to one ring carbon, and another from that carbon's bond to the ring bromine back onto the bromine.",
    "Right: 1,2-dibromoethane. The two bromines arrived from opposite faces."
  ],
